## MCP Tools

This repo's stack classification (mandatory-supported / candidate-supported / weak / unsupported) is the authoritative source for whether `code-review-graph` is required, advisory, or best-effort here. Read it once from `rules/operational/graph-supported-stacks.md` (single source of truth) — do not infer the policy from this template, which only applies as an example baseline.

If this stack is currently classified mandatory-supported, `setup.sh` and `setup-repos.sh` hard-fail when the graph cannot be installed/built and this section MUST NOT be deleted. If classified weak or candidate, the graph runs best-effort and any output is auto-prefixed `[INTELLISENSE-WEAK-STACK: <stack> — <reason>]`.

When the graph is available, prefer it over Grep/Read for these engineering questions (defined in `rules/operational/intelligence-sources.md` — refer to that file for the current set):
- `change-spread`, `who-calls`, `what-changed-and-where-does-it-spread`

Fall back to Grep/Read for raw text/regex search or when the graph does not cover the query.

## Task Tracking

Every Tier 1+ commit carries its own task reference, with a key from `evidence.task_trailers` in `.intellisense.yml`
(`Task: PROJ-12`, `Refs: #123`, or `Beads: <id>` when the team tracks work in Beads); the change also carries one
change contract. The pre-push hook and CI block a Tier 1+ push without them. Run `intellisense classify` to see a
change's tier.

Tier 0 (docs, tests, comment-only edits) needs neither. Anything touching API/DB/auth/financial/migration/cross-service
is Tier 1+; the policy's path rules decide.

